### The Snowflake Connector for Python is pure Python on this platform, and the
### whole point of this port is to make sure it stays that way.
###
### Upstream ships a C++ result-set decoder: a vendored copy of nanoarrow plus
### flatcc and 24 converter sources, reached through a Cython module called
### snowflake.connector.nanoarrow_arrow_iterator. It decodes the Arrow-format
### result chunks Snowflake returns. It is genuinely optional -- setup.py skips
### it when SNOWFLAKE_DISABLE_COMPILE_ARROW_EXTENSIONS is set, and cursor.py
### catches the ImportError, sets CAN_USE_ARROW_RESULT_FORMAT to False and asks
### the server for JSON instead:
###
###   if not CAN_USE_ARROW_RESULT_FORMAT:
###       logger.debug("Cannot use arrow result format, fallback to json format")
###       statement_params = {PARAMETER_PYTHON_CONNECTOR_QUERY_RESULT_FORMAT: "JSON"}
###
### so a connector without it is a working connector, not a crippled one. That
### is the build this port produces, and the resulting wheel is
### py3-none-any: one artifact for every interpreter.
###
### Why disable it rather than try. Two reasons, in order of importance:
###
###   1. Arrow's IPC format is little-endian by definition and this is a
###      big-endian machine, so every buffer has to be byte-swapped on the way
###      in. nanoarrow knows this -- ArrowIpcDecoderNeedsSwapEndian() compares
###      the stream's endianness against the system's -- so the path exists and
###      is not obviously wrong. It is simply not a path anyone has run here,
###      and the failure mode of getting it subtly wrong is silently incorrect
###      query results. That is the worst possible bug in a database driver and
###      it is not worth risking for a decode speedup.
###
###   2. Nothing needs it. JSON results are slower to parse, not less correct.
###
### Turning it on later is a contained change: set the variable to false, add
### cython>=3.1 to the build venv, and prove the swap path with a round-trip
### against a real account. Until someone does that, this stays off.
###
### What pip does without this port, and why the port exists at all. PyPI has
### no py3-none-any wheel for this package -- only manylinux/macos/windows
### binaries -- so pip on z/OS falls through to the sdist. The sdist's
### [build-system] requires names cython>=3.1, an isolated build installs it,
### _ABLE_TO_COMPILE_EXTENSIONS comes out true, and pip tries to compile the
### C++ decoder. This port is what stops that happening by accident.
###
### cffi is the other thing that bites. cryptography needs it, there is no
### buildable cffi on this platform (libffi has no XPLINK backend), and the
### only z/OS cffi is the prebuilt wheel on the zopen index. PyPI always has a
### newer cffi, pip prefers newest, and the newest resolves to an sdist that
### dies on a missing ffi.h. Every pip call below therefore passes
### --only-binary=:all:, which makes pip skip any version it cannot get a
### compatible wheel for and fall back to the one the index carries. That is
### deliberately not a version pin: a pin goes stale the day the index moves.

SNOWFLAKE_VERSION="4.7.2"

# bump: snowflake-version /SNOWFLAKE_VERSION="(.*)"/ https://github.com/snowflakedb/snowflake-connector-python.git|semver:*

export ZOPEN_STABLE_URL="https://github.com/snowflakedb/snowflake-connector-python.git"
export ZOPEN_STABLE_TAG="v${SNOWFLAKE_VERSION}"
export ZOPEN_STABLE_DEPS="check_python curl git"

export ZOPEN_DEV_URL="https://github.com/snowflakedb/snowflake-connector-python.git"
export ZOPEN_DEV_DEPS="${ZOPEN_STABLE_DEPS}"

# cryptography is the one runtime dependency that is not pure Python: it is a
# Rust extension, built per interpreter, and shipped by its own zopen port.
# Everything else this connector needs is pure Python and is bundled by
# zopen_post_install below -- see the note there for why the split falls here.
export ZOPEN_RUNTIME_DEPS="check_python cryptography"

export ZOPEN_CATEGORIES="development library database"

export ZOPEN_PYTHON_VERSIONS="${ZOPEN_PYTHON_VERSIONS:-3.12 3.13 3.14}"
export ZOPEN_BUILD_SYSTEM="Python"

# Nothing here is compiled, and without this zopen-build still tries to build
# its zoslib environment hooks with an unset compiler:
#
#   -DPATH_MAX=1024: eval: ... FSUM7351 not found
#   ***ERROR: Compiler command for zoslib_env_hook.c failed to compile
export ZOPEN_COMP="skip"

# The switch this whole port turns on. setup.py reads it as a string and
# accepts y/yes/t/true/1/on; anything else leaves the extension enabled.
export SNOWFLAKE_DISABLE_COMPILE_ARROW_EXTENSIONS="true"

# Build in the venv rather than a fresh PyPI-resolved environment. Isolation
# would fetch cython>=3.1 from PyPI purely to satisfy [build-system], which is
# both pointless -- the variable above means it goes unused -- and the one
# thing that could quietly re-enable the C++ decoder if that variable were ever
# dropped. Not installing cython is the belt to the variable's braces.
export ZOPEN_PYTHON_BUILD_ISOLATION="false"

# Required consequence of the above: without isolation, 'build' still validates
# [build-system] requires against the venv and would stop on the missing
# cython. The requirement is real for the extension build and irrelevant to
# this one.
export ZOPEN_PYTHON_BUILD_SKIP_DEP_CHECK="true"

zopen_get_version() {
  echo "${SNOWFLAKE_VERSION}"
}

# Drop the prebuilt native libraries upstream ships for other platforms.
#
# The connector vendors a small native library called minicore, as a directory
# per platform: aix_ppc64, linux_{x86_64,aarch64}_{glibc,musl}, macos_{x86_64,
# aarch64}, windows_x86_64. None of them is z/OS and none of them can load
# here. setup.py knows to strip the ones it does not need -- PlatformBuildPy
# calls _prune_minicore() -- but it gives up when it cannot identify the
# platform it is on:
#
#     keep = _minicore_native_subdir()
#     if keep is None:
#         return
#
# and _minicore_native_subdir() returns None for any machine that is not
# x86_64, aarch64 or ppc64. So on z/OS the "keep only the native one" path
# turns into "keep all nine", and the wheel comes out at 14 MB instead of
# 1.2 MB -- most of it an AIX static archive of 17 MB uncompressed.
#
# Removing the platform directories rather than patching the pruner keeps this
# independent of how upstream spells the check. The package directory and its
# __init__.py stay, because the loader reaches minicore through
# importlib.resources.files("snowflake.connector.minicore") and an absent
# package would turn a miss into an import error.
#
# Nothing needs the library. snowflake/connector/__init__.py already treats it
# as optional:
#
#     try:
#         _core_loader.load()
#     except Exception:
#         # This ensures the connector module loads even if the minicore
#         # library is unavailable
#         pass
#
# so a minicore with no matching platform is a supported state, not a broken
# one. The "installed connector is pure Python" check asserts the result.
zopen_pre_build() {
  # Do not assume the working directory. It is the source tree by the time the
  # build itself runs, but this hook is earlier and a wrong guess here would
  # silently prune nothing -- which is exactly the failure this is preventing.
  _mc_root=""
  for _mc_cand in "${PWD}" "${ZOPEN_ROOT}/${sourcedir}"; do
    if [ -d "${_mc_cand}/src/snowflake/connector/minicore" ]; then
      _mc_root="${_mc_cand}"
      break
    fi
  done

  if [ -z "${_mc_root}" ]; then
    # Upstream may have stopped shipping these, in which case there is nothing
    # to do -- but say so, because the alternative is that the tree moved and
    # this hook is quietly a no-op.
    printInfo "- no minicore directory found; nothing to prune"
    return 0
  fi

  _mc_dir="${_mc_root}/src/snowflake/connector/minicore"
  _mc_removed=0
  for _mc_entry in "${_mc_dir}"/*; do
    [ -d "${_mc_entry}" ] || continue
    case "$(basename "${_mc_entry}")" in
      __*) continue ;;
    esac
    rm -rf "${_mc_entry}" || {
      printSoftError "Could not remove ${_mc_entry}"
      return 1
    }
    _mc_removed=$((_mc_removed + 1))
  done

  if [ ! -f "${_mc_dir}/__init__.py" ]; then
    printSoftError "minicore/__init__.py is missing; the resource lookup would fail"
    return 1
  fi

  printInfo "- pruned ${_mc_removed} non-z/OS minicore platform director(ies)"
  return 0
}

# The connector's direct runtime dependencies, as its own metadata gives them.
# Used by the check, where pip resolves the closure itself and cryptography is
# meant to be pulled in.
#
# boto3/botocore are in this list because Requires-Dist has them, not because
# importing the connector needs them -- options.py routes both through
# MissingOptionalDependency, so their absence surfaces only if you actually
# transfer to an S3-backed stage. They are also most of the bundle's size
# (botocore alone is ~16 MB). If that ever matters more than completeness,
# dropping them is safe and the failure it introduces is a clear one.
_snowflake_runtime_deps() {
  echo "asn1crypto<2.0.0,>0.24.0" \
       "pyOpenSSL>=24.0.0" \
       "pyjwt<3.0.0,>=2.10.1" \
       "pytz" \
       "requests<3.0.0,>=2.32.4" \
       "packaging" \
       "charset_normalizer<4,>=2" \
       "idna<4,>=3.7" \
       "certifi>=2024.7.4" \
       "typing_extensions<5,>=4.3" \
       "filelock<4,>=3.5" \
       "sortedcontainers>=2.4.0" \
       "platformdirs<5.0.0,>=2.6.0" \
       "tomlkit" \
       "boto3>=1.24" \
       "botocore>=1.24"
}

# What the bundle installs, which is deliberately not the list above.
#
# The bundle is built with --no-deps and therefore has to name the whole pure
# closure itself, transitive entries included. Letting pip resolve it instead
# looks simpler and is wrong: pyOpenSSL depends on cryptography, cryptography
# depends on cffi, and both are compiled, so a resolving install quietly drops
# two per-interpreter .so files into a tree three interpreters share --
#
#   the bundle contains 2 compiled object(s); it must be pure Python
#     .../lib/python/_cffi_backend.cpython-312.so
#     .../lib/python/cryptography/hazmat/bindings/_rust.cpython-312.so
#
# which is the purity assertion below doing its job. Naming the closure makes
# the bundle's contents explicit and auditable, and keeps cryptography coming
# from its own port for every interpreter rather than from here for one.
#
# The five names that are not in the list above are all transitive: urllib3 and
# certifi via requests, jmespath and s3transfer via boto3, python-dateutil and
# six via botocore. Versions are left to pip except where the connector itself
# specifies one.
_snowflake_bundle_deps() {
  echo "asn1crypto<2.0.0,>0.24.0" \
       "pyOpenSSL>=24.0.0" \
       "pyjwt<3.0.0,>=2.10.1" \
       "pytz" \
       "requests<3.0.0,>=2.32.4" \
       "packaging" \
       "charset_normalizer<4,>=2" \
       "idna<4,>=3.7" \
       "certifi>=2024.7.4" \
       "typing_extensions<5,>=4.3" \
       "filelock<4,>=3.5" \
       "sortedcontainers>=2.4.0" \
       "platformdirs<5.0.0,>=2.6.0" \
       "tomlkit" \
       "boto3>=1.24" \
       "botocore>=1.24" \
       "urllib3" \
       "jmespath" \
       "s3transfer" \
       "python-dateutil" \
       "six"
}

# zopen-build installs the wheel with --no-deps, so the check venv has the
# connector and nothing it needs. Install that here rather than in a build
# hook: this runs once per interpreter with that interpreter's venv already
# active, which is exactly the environment the checks are supposed to describe.
zopen_python_test() {
  # cryptography comes from the zopen index; everything else is a pure wheel on
  # PyPI. --only-binary=:all: is what keeps cffi resolvable -- see the note at
  # the top of this file.
  # shellcheck disable=SC2046
  pip install --disable-pip-version-check --quiet --only-binary=:all: \
    --extra-index-url https://repo.zopen.community/pypi/wheels/simple/ \
    $(_snowflake_runtime_deps) "cryptography>=46.0.5" \
    || { printSoftError "Could not install the connector's runtime dependencies"; return 1; }

  SNOWFLAKE_EXPECTED_VERSION="${SNOWFLAKE_VERSION}" python - <<'PYEOF'
import os
import sys

# Deliberately not the wording zopen-build prints for its own per-interpreter
# header: zopen_check_results counts that header to notice an interpreter that
# died before its first check, and a second line matching it would be read as
# a crash that never happened.
print("Interpreter: Python %s" % sys.version.split()[0])

# zopen builds PYTHONPATH as "${PYTHONPATH}:..." from a variable that starts
# unset, leaving a leading empty entry that Python expands to the working
# directory -- which during the check is the source tree, and the source tree
# has a src/snowflake/ in it.
sys.path = [p for p in sys.path if p not in ("", os.getcwd())]

passed = 0
failed = 0


def check(name, fn):
    global passed, failed
    try:
        fn()
    except Exception as exc:
        print("FAIL: %s -- %s: %s" % (name, type(exc).__name__, exc))
        failed += 1
    else:
        print("PASS: %s" % name)
        passed += 1


import snowflake.connector as sc

expected = os.environ["SNOWFLAKE_EXPECTED_VERSION"]


def version_is_ours():
    if sc.__version__ != expected:
        raise AssertionError("imported %s, this port builds %s"
                             % (sc.__version__, expected))


check("version is the one this port builds", version_is_ours)


# The claim this port is built on. If the C++ decoder ever does get compiled in
# by accident -- a dropped environment variable, an isolated build that pulled
# cython -- this is the check that says so, and it says so before anyone runs a
# query whose numbers come back byte-swapped.
def arrow_extension_is_absent():
    import importlib.util
    spec = importlib.util.find_spec("snowflake.connector.nanoarrow_arrow_iterator")
    if spec is not None:
        raise AssertionError(
            "the nanoarrow C++ extension is present at %s; this port is meant to "
            "build without it and its byte-swap path is unverified on this "
            "platform" % (spec.origin,))


check("the Arrow C++ extension was not compiled in", arrow_extension_is_absent)


def falls_back_to_json():
    from snowflake.connector.cursor import CAN_USE_ARROW_RESULT_FORMAT
    if CAN_USE_ARROW_RESULT_FORMAT:
        raise AssertionError("CAN_USE_ARROW_RESULT_FORMAT is True without the "
                             "extension, so the JSON fallback will not engage")


check("the connector falls back to the JSON result format", falls_back_to_json)


def is_pure_python():
    import snowflake.connector
    root = os.path.dirname(snowflake.connector.__file__)
    found = []
    for dirpath, _dirnames, filenames in os.walk(root):
        for name in filenames:
            if name.endswith((".so", ".x")):
                found.append(os.path.join(dirpath, name))
    if found:
        raise AssertionError("compiled objects in an installed tree that should "
                             "be pure Python: %s" % ", ".join(found))


check("the installed connector is pure Python", is_pure_python)


# A wheel that imports but cannot present the DB-API surface is no use, and
# these are the names every caller and every ORM adapter reaches for first.
def dbapi_surface():
    for attr in ("connect", "apilevel", "threadsafety", "paramstyle",
                 "Error", "DatabaseError", "ProgrammingError",
                 "OperationalError", "IntegrityError", "InterfaceError"):
        if not hasattr(sc, attr):
            raise AssertionError("missing DB-API attribute %r" % attr)
    if sc.apilevel != "2.0":
        raise AssertionError("apilevel is %r, expected '2.0'" % sc.apilevel)


check("the DB-API 2.0 surface is present", dbapi_surface)


# Everything below is reachable without a Snowflake account, which is the
# point: these are the parts of the connector that this platform could plausibly
# break, exercised without needing credentials in CI.
def type_converter_round_trips():
    from snowflake.connector.converter import SnowflakeConverter
    conv = SnowflakeConverter()
    # to_snowflake is the direction that turns Python values into bind
    # parameters, and it is where a byte-order or encoding fault would show.
    for value in (1, -1, 2 ** 40, 3.5, "text", True):
        fn = conv.to_snowflake_bindings(
            conv.snowflake_type(value), value)
        if fn is None:
            raise AssertionError("no binding for %r" % (value,))


check("the type converter binds Python values", type_converter_round_trips)


def big_endian_int_binding():
    # Snowflake binds integers as decimal strings, so a big-endian machine must
    # produce the same text a little-endian one would. A value wider than 32
    # bits is the one that catches a struct/byte-order mistake.
    from snowflake.connector.converter import SnowflakeConverter
    conv = SnowflakeConverter()
    value = 1234567890123456789
    fn = conv.to_snowflake_bindings(conv.snowflake_type(value), value)
    if str(fn) != str(value):
        raise AssertionError("bound %r as %r" % (value, fn))


check("a 64-bit integer binds identically on big-endian", big_endian_int_binding)


def byte_order_is_big():
    if sys.byteorder != "big":
        raise AssertionError("expected a big-endian interpreter, got %s"
                             % sys.byteorder)


check("native byte order is big-endian", byte_order_is_big)


# Key-pair authentication is the mechanism most likely to be used from a batch
# job on this platform, and it is the one that depends on the ported
# cryptography and on jwt. Generating a key and signing a token exercises both
# without contacting Snowflake.
def keypair_auth_primitives():
    from cryptography.hazmat.primitives import serialization
    from cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric import rsa
    import jwt

    key = rsa.generate_private_key(public_exponent=65537, key_size=2048)
    der = key.private_bytes(serialization.Encoding.DER,
                            serialization.PrivateFormat.PKCS8,
                            serialization.NoEncryption())
    if len(der) < 512:
        raise AssertionError("PKCS8 DER is implausibly short: %d bytes" % len(der))
    token = jwt.encode({"sub": "zopen"}, "secret", algorithm="HS256")
    if jwt.decode(token, "secret", algorithms=["HS256"])["sub"] != "zopen":
        raise AssertionError("JWT did not round-trip")


check("key-pair auth primitives work", keypair_auth_primitives)


def tls_trust_store_is_readable():
    # The connector will not open a connection it cannot verify, and a missing
    # or unreadable CA bundle is a failure that only shows at connect time.
    import certifi
    path = certifi.where()
    with open(path, "rb") as fh:
        blob = fh.read()
    if b"BEGIN CERTIFICATE" not in blob:
        raise AssertionError("no PEM certificates in %s" % path)


check("the CA bundle is present and readable", tls_trust_store_is_readable)


def optional_deps_degrade():
    # boto3 is declared but routed through MissingOptionalDependency. Whether
    # it is installed or not, importing the module that uses it must not raise.
    import snowflake.connector.platform_detection  # noqa: F401


check("optional dependencies degrade instead of raising", optional_deps_degrade)

print("snowflake-connector-python %s on Python %s -- %d passed, %d failed"
      % (sc.__version__, sys.version.split()[0], passed, failed))
sys.exit(1 if failed else 0)
PYEOF
}

zopen_check_results() {
  dir="$1"; pfx="$2"; chk="${dir}/${pfx}_check.log"
  # No "|| echo 0": grep -c prints 0 when nothing matches and also exits 1, so
  # the fallback appends a second zero and the arithmetic dies.
  passed=$(grep -c "^PASS:" "${chk}" 2> /dev/null)
  failed=$(grep -c "^FAIL:" "${chk}" 2> /dev/null)
  passed=${passed:-0}
  failed=${failed:-0}

  # An interpreter whose script dies before the first check contributes no PASS
  # and no FAIL, so counting those alone would score a crash as a clean run.
  interpreters=$(grep -c "Running tests with Python" "${chk}" 2> /dev/null)
  reported=$(grep -c "^snowflake-connector-python .* on Python" "${chk}" 2> /dev/null)
  interpreters=${interpreters:-0}
  reported=${reported:-0}
  silent=$((interpreters - reported))
  if [ "${silent}" -gt 0 ]; then
    failed=$((failed + silent))
  fi

cat <<ZZ
actualFailures:${failed}
totalTests:$((passed + failed))
expectedFailures:0
ZZ
}

# The connector's pure-Python dependencies are not zopen ports and are not on
# the wheel index, so an installed tree without them cannot import. Bundling
# them here is safe for exactly the reason it is safe in the other Python
# ports: this runs once, under the primary interpreter, into a lib/python that
# every interpreter this port serves shares -- which is only sound while every
# bundled file is pure Python. That is asserted below rather than assumed.
#
# cryptography is deliberately not bundled. It is a Rust extension with a
# per-interpreter ABI, so one copy cannot serve three interpreters; it is a
# zopen port and is declared in ZOPEN_RUNTIME_DEPS instead.
zopen_post_install() {
  printHeader "Bundling the connector's pure-Python runtime dependencies"

  # Resolve an interpreter and call pip through it. This hook runs with no venv
  # active, so a bare `pip` is not on PATH and the failure says nothing about
  # why:
  #
  #   pip: zopen_post_install 8: install 24: ... FSUM7351 not found
  _pi_py=$(_zopen_python_interpreter_path "$(_zopen_python_versions | head -1 | awk '{print $1}')" 2> /dev/null)
  [ -x "${_pi_py}" ] || _pi_py=$(command -v python3 2> /dev/null)
  if [ ! -x "${_pi_py}" ]; then
    printSoftError "No interpreter available to bundle the runtime dependencies"
    return 1
  fi
  printInfo "- bundling with ${_pi_py}"

  # --no-deps, with the closure named explicitly: see _snowflake_bundle_deps.
  # shellcheck disable=SC2046
  "${_pi_py}" -m pip install --disable-pip-version-check --quiet --only-binary=:all: \
    --no-deps \
    --target="${ZOPEN_INSTALL_DIR}/lib/python" \
    $(_snowflake_bundle_deps) \
    || { printSoftError "Could not bundle the runtime dependencies"; return 1; }

  # Anything compiled would have been built for one interpreter and imported by
  # three. This is the assertion the paragraph above depends on.
  _pi_ext=$(find "${ZOPEN_INSTALL_DIR}/lib/python" -name "*.so" -o -name "*.x" 2> /dev/null | wc -l | tr -d ' ')
  if [ "${_pi_ext}" != "0" ]; then
    printSoftError "the bundle contains ${_pi_ext} compiled object(s); it must be pure Python"
    find "${ZOPEN_INSTALL_DIR}/lib/python" \( -name "*.so" -o -name "*.x" \) 2> /dev/null \
      | while read -r _pi_bad; do printSoftError "  ${_pi_bad}"; done
    return 1
  fi
  printInfo "- bundle is pure Python"

  # The check that does not care how any of them is laid out on disk: they have
  # to import. Not every distribution name is its module name, so this lists
  # modules, not requirements.
  if [ -x "${_pi_py}" ]; then
    # pyOpenSSL is deliberately not in this list. It cannot import without
    # cryptography, which is not in the bundle by design, so importing it here
    # would test whether the cryptography port happens to be installed on the
    # build machine rather than whether this bundle is sound. The check phase
    # covers it, in a venv where cryptography is present.
    _pi_mods='import asn1crypto, jwt, pytz, requests, packaging, charset_normalizer, idna, certifi, typing_extensions, filelock, sortedcontainers, platformdirs, tomlkit, boto3, botocore, urllib3, jmespath, s3transfer, dateutil, six'
    if PYTHONPATH="${ZOPEN_INSTALL_DIR}/lib/python" "${_pi_py}" -c "${_pi_mods}" 2> /dev/null; then
      printInfo "- every bundled dependency imports from the bundle"
    else
      printSoftError "the bundled modules do not import from ${ZOPEN_INSTALL_DIR}/lib/python"
      PYTHONPATH="${ZOPEN_INSTALL_DIR}/lib/python" "${_pi_py}" -c "${_pi_mods}" 2>&1 \
        | while read -r _pi_line; do printSoftError "  ${_pi_line}"; done
      return 1
    fi
  else
    printSoftError "no interpreter available to verify the bundle imports"
  fi
  return 0
}

# zopen-build captures stdout from here into metadata.json as installation
# caveats. This is the only place a user who never reads the README is told
# what they are getting, so it says the two things that change behaviour.
zopen_install_caveats() {
  cat <<'CAVEATEOF'
Query results come back as JSON, not Arrow.

This connector is built without its optional C++ result-set decoder, so it asks
Snowflake for the JSON result format. Everything works; large result sets parse
more slowly than they would elsewhere. Nothing needs configuring -- the fallback
is automatic and the connector logs it at debug level:

    Cannot use arrow result format, fallback to json format

The reason is byte order. Arrow's IPC format is little-endian by definition and
this is a big-endian machine, so every buffer needs swapping on the way in. That
path exists in nanoarrow but has never been exercised here, and a decoder that
is subtly wrong returns wrong numbers rather than an error. It is off until
someone verifies it against a real account.

Consequences worth knowing:

  * fetch_pandas_all() and fetch_pandas_batches() are unavailable. They are
    Arrow-only, and raise NotSupportedError rather than returning bad data.
    Use fetchall() and build the DataFrame yourself.
  * write_pandas() is unaffected.

Installing with pip rather than zopen needs two things, because there is no
buildable cffi on this platform and PyPI always has a newer one than the
prebuilt wheel:

    export PIP_EXTRA_INDEX_URL="https://repo.zopen.community/pypi/wheels/simple/"
    pip install --only-binary=:all: snowflake-connector-python

--only-binary=:all: is what makes pip skip the versions it has no z/OS wheel for
instead of trying to compile them.
CAVEATEOF
}
